{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For only Og king of Bashan remained of the remnant of the Rephaim; behold, his bed is a bed of iron, etc.</b> The meaning of the verse is that it comes to tell that this Og was called king of the Amorite because he ruled over them, but he himself was from the Rephaim. And it comes to tell of his height and his greatness, for the Rephaim were a great and tall people like the Anakim. It says that his bed is a bed of iron — a bed of wood would not support him as for other men — and it is in Rabbah, which was his city of residence. And when the children of Ammon destroyed the Rephaim, as mentioned above (Deuteronomy 2:21), and his city was captured, he alone escaped from their hand. And this is what is said: 'For only Og remained of the remnant of the Rephaim.' And behold, his bed remains there in Rabbah, which now belongs to the children of Ammon, and the Ammonites preserved it as testimony that they destroyed a great and tall people, and the mighty man whose height was like the height of cedards, mighty like the oaks — they took his kingdom from him. And this is the meaning of <b>behold</b> — that is, behold, the testimony stands: it is a bed of iron, and very long and very wide, and it stands in Rabbath of the children of Ammon. For his bed that was in Ashtaroth had become spoil and was lost." "And the meaning of <b>by the cubit of a man</b> is the great one among men, as in 'and be strong, and be a man' (I Kings 2:2). And according to Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 3:11), באמת אישׁ means 'by the cubit of the man,' and the translation is מלך (king). And so את הכבש אחד (Numbers 28:4), בלילה הוא (Genesis 19:33), and many similar ones — that is, by the cubit of Og himself."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:13:55.116430386Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}